    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how many students did not receive an A in either history or math, we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion and set theory.\n\nGiven:\n- Total number of students = 30\n- Number of students who received an A in history (H) = 7\n- Number of students who received an A in math (M) = 13\n- Number of students who received an A in both history and math (H ∩ M) = 4\n\nLet's define the following sets:\n- \\( |H| = 7 \\) (students who received an A in history)\n- \\( |M| = 13 \\) (students who received an A in math)\n- \\( |H \\cap M| = 4 \\) (students who received an A in both history and math)\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received an A in either history or math or both. This can be calculated using the principle of inclusion-exclusion:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nSo, 16 students received an A in either history or math or both. \n\nTo find the number of students who did not receive an A in either course, we subtract the number of students who received an A from the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of students without an A in either course} = 30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of students who did not receive an A in either history or math is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
